###### 123.12 — Motion for recorded vote — House

A motion for a recorded vote on a question that could lead to a recorded division must be moved by one representative of a recognized party, who must inform the House of the party’s position.

###### 123.13 — Recorded vote — House

When the House votes on a motion, the Clerk calls for the yeas and nays. The yeas and nays are recorded on the list of votes and proceedings.

###### 123.14 — Division of votes — House

When the House votes on a motion, the Clerk calls for the yeas and nays. If there are two or more votes, the Clerk shall first call for the yeas and then call for the nays.

###### 123.15 — Voting

The following rules apply when the House votes on a motion:

(a) the Clerk shall first call for the yeas and then call for the nays;

(b) the Clerk shall repeat the motion before calling for the yeas and nays;

(c) the Clerk shall call for the yeas and nays in the order in which the members are listed in the list of members;

(d) if the member whose name is called is absent or unable to vote, the Clerk shall call for the next name;

